{
  "sentences": [
    {
      "sentenceNo": 1,
      "english": "It's a topic people usually avoid, yet it's a universal human experience."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 2,
      "english": "Flatulence, affectionately known as farting, is simply the releasing of gas from the backside."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 3,
      "english": "The word itself traces back to the Latin 'flatus', which literally means 'a blowing' or 'wind.'"
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 4,
      "english": "While it is often the butt of the joke, we do it for very practical reasons, which boil down to digestion."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 5,
      "english": "Most mammals have a complex, symbiotic relationship with their gut microbes which break down food."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 6,
      "english": "Digestion requires a lengthy process of fermentation, and flatulence is simply the inevitable byproduct of these hard-working bacteria."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 7,
      "english": "Passing gas is a sign of a highly functioning digestive system."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 8,
      "english": "But we humans aren't the only mammals that like to let one off."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 9,
      "english": "In 2017, National Geographic and the BBC reported the results of an online survey."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 10,
      "english": "It revealed some incredibly unlikely farters, like zebras, which have been documented farting with every single step whilst running away from predators."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 11,
      "english": "On a bigger scale, cow flatulence and burps are packed with methane, a potent greenhouse gas that is actively driving global climate change."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 12,
      "english": "And, if you've ever had a domesticated pet, you might've even blamed your own farts on them!"
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 13,
      "english": "I know I have."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 14,
      "english": "There are other fascinating examples of farting from the animal kingdom."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 15,
      "english": "The Sonoran coral snake expels air forcefully to startle predators when it's in danger."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 16,
      "english": "In an article published on The Conversation, it's mentioned that herring, a type of fish, will release gas as a way to communicate with each other that predators are nearby."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 17,
      "english": "It was also written in IFLScience that the beaded lacewing, a type of larva, can take down six termites with one fart!"
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 18,
      "english": "So it seems that some animals pass gas as an evolutionary survival tactic."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 19,
      "english": "Those not so 'silent but deadly' farts can ward off predators as well as let others know that there's potential danger."
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 20,
      "english": "While farts for us are mainly the result of digestion, perhaps, like the Sonoran snake, herring and beaded lacewing, we could embrace the breeze and utilise them against our enemies?"
    },
    {
      "sentenceNo": 21,
      "english": "Hahaha, just joking!"
    }
  ],
